I have been pretty frustrated with the state of NetHack and its patches
as they have arrived at my system.  In order to get things to work, I
have had several choices, and what I have done is to go back to the
saved articles, edit them by hand, and try again.

I will repeat: I think things like this should have brik-style checksums
so that we can tell whether the patch is okay before we mess up a huge
directory with broken, partial, patches.

Anyway, I have developed a small suite of things that help me maintain
this stuff on my UNIX box.  They are contained in a shar at the end of
this article.

Here's how it works:  I keep all the original newarticles in a single
directory, in files named hack.* (for the original sources) and nh3.p**
where **=patchnumber,fileletter.  The so-called TopLevel directory is
a subdirectory of the one with the news articles.

I have three shell scripts: loadup, checkout, and cleanpat
Plus, when the thing says "check for rejects", I enter the command
	file . -print|fgrep '#"
which shows me any reject files.

'loadup' (invoked in the subdirectory as ../loadup) unshars and applies
patches.  It sends all the output to a file called 'out', so that I can
browse the results.

'checkout' (../checkout) browses for the kinds of problems I am used to
seeing.  It lets me know what's broken (ususally).  The 'out' file is
now pushing 400K, a bit much for me to scan visually and expect to see
what needs to be seen.

'cleanpat' (../cleanpat) finds all the '~' files and deletes them, so
I can save time and space in archiving the results.

This has worked pretty well for me, and I think I have fixed the damage
done to the articles before they got to me, except that there are some
offsets to patches applied to unixconf.h and do_wear.c.

In the meanwhile, I have had to exchange lines, add control characters,
and delete added newlines in the files to make the patches work.  A lot
of work for a game that is still to hard for me.

#! /bin/sh
# This is a shell archive.  Remove anything before this line, then unpack
# it by saving it into a file and typing "sh file".  To overwrite existing
# files, type "sh file -c".  You can also feed this as standard input via
# unshar, or by typing "sh <file", e.g..  If this archive is complete, you
# will see the following message at the end:
#		"End of shell archive."
# Contents:  loadup checkout cleanpat
# Wrapped by kevin@kosman on Sun Nov 26 14:25:43 1989
PATH=/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/ucb ; export PATH
if test -f 'loadup' -a "${1}" != "-c" ; then 
  echo shar: Will not clobber existing file \"'loadup'\"
else
echo shar: Extracting \"'loadup'\" \(809 characters\)
sed "s/^X//" >'loadup' <<'END_OF_FILE'
Xecho '{Loading original distribution}' >out
Xunshar ../hack* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Loading patch 1}' >>out
Xunshar ../nh3.p1* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Applying patch 1}' >>out
Xsh do_patch.sh 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Loading patch 2}' >>out
Xunshar ../nh3.p2* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Applying patch 2}' >>out
Xsh do_patch2.sh 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Loading patch 3}' >>out
Xunshar ../nh3.p3* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Applying patch 3}' >>out
Xsh do_patch3.sh 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Loading patch 4}' >>out
Xunshar ../nh3.p4* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Applying patch 4}' >>out
Xsh do_patch4.sh 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Loading patch 5}' >>out
Xunshar ../nh3.p5* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Applying patch 5}' >>out
Xsh do_patch5.sh 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Loading patch 6}' >>out
Xunshar ../nh3.p6* 1>>out 2>&1
X
Xecho '{Applying patch 6}' >>out
Xsh do_patch6.sh 1>>out 2>&1
END_OF_FILE
if test 809 -ne `wc -c <'loadup'`; then
    echo shar: \"'loadup'\" unpacked with wrong size!
fi
chmod +x 'loadup'
# end of 'loadup'
fi
if test -f 'checkout' -a "${1}" != "-c" ; then 
  echo shar: Will not clobber existing file \"'checkout'\"
else
echo shar: Extracting \"'checkout'\" \(106 characters\)
sed "s/^X//" >'checkout' <<'END_OF_FILE'
X# checkout - check the 'out' file for obvious problems
X
Xfgrep 'wrong
Xfail
Xfuzz
X{
X}
XMalformed
Xoffset' out
X
END_OF_FILE
if test 106 -ne `wc -c <'checkout'`; then
    echo shar: \"'checkout'\" unpacked with wrong size!
fi
chmod +x 'checkout'
# end of 'checkout'
fi
if test -f 'cleanpat' -a "${1}" != "-c" ; then 
  echo shar: Will not clobber existing file \"'cleanpat'\"
else
echo shar: Extracting \"'cleanpat'\" \(58 characters\)
sed "s/^X//" >'cleanpat' <<'END_OF_FILE'
Xfind . -print|fgrep '~'| ( while read i ; do rm $i; done)
END_OF_FILE
if test 58 -ne `wc -c <'cleanpat'`; then
    echo shar: \"'cleanpat'\" unpacked with wrong size!
fi
chmod +x 'cleanpat'
# end of 'cleanpat'
fi
echo shar: End of shell archive.
exit 0


